Donald G. Marchlenski (march@ohstpy.mps.ohio-state.edu) wrote:
: I am running DU 4.0a

: I tried to modify a user's default shell to ksh by editing the /etc/passwd
: file but the change wasn't recognized when the user logged in and he was
: still in the bourne shell.  I had to run the GUI account management program
: from the CDE to make the change stick.  There is a file called
: /etc/passwd.pag that apparently has something to do with this.

: Does the GUI system management keep me from simply managing my system
: through the old methods of editing files, etc.?

: /etc/passwd.pag is a binary file that I assume must be changed by the
: CDE account manager program.

Don,

I suspect you edited the /etc/passwd file directly (e.g. vi /etc/passwd).
If so, you are correct that the changes were not propogated to the 
/etc/passwd.dir AND /etc/passwd.pag files. You can still edit the
/etc/passwd file to make quick changes, you just either need to:

a) run the mkpasswd(8) command to update the hashed password database
(/etc/passwd.dir and /etc/passwd.pag)

or

b) use the vipw(8) command to edit /etc/passwd and when you exit from
vi, the hashed password database file will be automatically updated.

Hope this helps.
Matt
