Chet Ramey wrote:
> 
> In article <338BF0E4.41C6@dcs.rhbnc.ac.uk>,
> Bob Vickers  <bobv@dcs.rhbnc.ac.uk> wrote:
> 
> >As far as I can make out, /bin/getopts is a total waste of space,
> >written by someone who doesn't know the first law of Unix programming:
> >"A Unix process cannot alter the environment of its parent".
> 
> Don't be so harsh.  I assume that DEC is attempting to conform to the
> letter of POSIX.2, which requires that certain `regular' builtins
> (of which `getopts' is one) ``be implemented in a manner so that
> they can be accessed via the POSIX.1 exec family of functions.''
> 
> POSIX.2 doesn't say they have to be useful, but they must be available.
> For your amusement, `read', `cd', `alias' and the job control builtins
> (fg/bg/jobs) must also be present in the file system as executables.
> --
> ``The lyf so short, the craft so long to lerne.'' - Chaucer
> 
> Chet Ramey, Case Western Reserve University     Internet: chet@po.CWRU.Edu

Yes, I was involved in the POSIX compliance effort at OSF and this is
exactly the case. POSIX requires that these commands be exec-able so we
put a little ksh script in /usr/bin which would run the ksh builtin of
the same name, and then placed links to it by names such as /usr/bin/cd,
/usr/bin/getopts, etc. Trust me, we were perfectly well aware of the
ridiculous nature of what we were doing. I can't speak for
implementations other than OSF/1 but presumably other vendors, at least
those who were as obsessive about standards as we were, did similar
things.

David



