In article <01bc7537$3cfa02c0$164645c0@tilly>, "Marc Tillinghast" <tilly@storm.com> writes:
|>
|>         0    
|>
|>If I type 'lmf reset' I get the following response:
|>
|># lmf reset
|>Not enough units to load OSF-BASE DEC
|>
|>What are these 'units'?  Is there a kernal parm I need to modify?
|>Any help would be appreciated.
|>
|>Marc
|>
|>

Marc,

The "units" should be specified in the license information that you entered.

The vaious hardware platforms have different requirements for the number
of units needed.  For example, a low-end workstation needs a license with less
units than a top-of-the-line 8400 system.  Therefore, a license for the 
workstation would not work on the 8400.

In your case, the license you have is apparently insufficient for the system
you are installing it on.  Certain licenses allow for the unit value to be
changed (you'll see something like "MOD_UNITS" in the license itself.)  You 
could try to input a larger unit value.  If you get a checksum error, you have
the type of license that won't accept a change.  In that case, you'll need a
new license for your system.

Regards,
Dave
