In article <5otp9p$kv2$1@news5.wavefront.com>,
Daniel A. Torrey <nospam@nospam.not> wrote:
> Can I trim the binary error log without rebooting, and still have the
> kernel write errors to it after I've trimmed it?  It's getting pretty big,
> and I'd like to zero it out, but I'm afraid that if I just nuke it, it
> won't be recreated until the next reboot.

Probably.  To be safe, I would kill `binlogd', cat /dev/null >
/var/adm/binary.errlog, and then restart `binlogd'.

Note: Don't try stopping binlogd by doing a

	/sbin/init.d/syslog stop

The script does stop syslog but it *doesn't* stop binlogd.  If you
subsequently try restarting binlogd by doing a

	/sbin/init.d/syslog start

you will have two copies of binlogd running.

Tim
