Donald G. Marchlenski wrote:
> 
> I tried to modify a user's default shell to ksh by editing the /etc/passwd file
> but the change wasn't recognized when the user logged in and he was still in
> the bourne shell.  I had to run the GUI account management program from the CDE
> to make the change stick.  There is a file called /etc/passwd.pag that
> apparently has something to do with this.
> 

When designing the CDE/SysMan management tools, we made a decision to
NEVER preclude the traditional methods of editing, command line
modification, etc. Unlike some of our rivals who create binary databases
of system information, we decided that was not acceptable. So we always
use the traditional underlying files that all of you know and love ;-)

In the case of accounts, the better way to propogate a change is to use
vipw to edit, or do a mkpasswd after the straight edit. This is a
security feature.



