From: Robin Rowe <rower@ise1.saic.com>
Subject: 100BASE-TX OSF Upgrade Procedure
Date: 1997/06/12
Message-ID: <33A09C54.5902@ise1.saic.com>
X-Deja-AN: 248006690
Organization: SAIC San Diego
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


I hesitate to post this since we haven't succeeded yet in finishing our
own upgrade, but below is what we believe to be the required procedure
to upgrade to a 100BASE-TX card on a DEC Alpha running OSF. We haven't
completed the upgrade ourselves because we still haven't installed the
required OS upgrade to 3.2G. 

The notes below were prepared by Andrew Obermeyer, the engineer on our
team who has actually been doing the work. 

DEC ALPHA OSF 100BASE-TX UPGRADE PROCEDURE
==========================================

The following process is used to install a 100 BasedT card as a second 
ethernet adapter in a DEC AlphaStation 600/266.

Prerequisites:
-----------
1.  Firmware version 3.9.  The firmware version is the release version.
This number can be found on the installation media.  There are several
firmwareversion that can be found at boot time, but a combination of
these make a release.  Version 3.9 is:
    Firmware revision: 6.4
    PALcode: OSF version 1.21
    ???
2.  OSF 3.2G or higher operating system.  To determine operating system
versionreboot machine and watch for the boot prompt.  You can use uname
-a, but this will give you the major operating system number and a
revision number. The revision numbers map somehow to the revision
letter.

Installation procedure:
----------------------
1.  Boot the generic kernel.  You must boot the generic kernel to
rebuild the new kernel with the new hardware.  New hardware will not be
recognized unless the generic kernel is booted.  During the boot process
you will notice standard output for the original tu0 and the new tu1
ethernet devices.

    b -fl s -fi /genvmunix

2.  Save current kernel.

    cp /vmunix /sys/TETRA/vmunix.save

    where TETRA is the name of the machine

3.  Rebuild the kernel.

    Default.  No hard coded modification needed for the kernel.

    1. Rebuild kernel
 
       doconfig

       <CR> to machine name
       y <CR> to confirm
       13 <CR> for rebuild all of the above
       y <CR> to confirm
       n <CR> no you don't want to edit configuration files. The kernel
          will now rebuild. Just wait this take a few minutes.
       
    2. Copy new kernel to root directory.

       cp /sys/TETRA/vmunix /vmunix

    Modification need from the default configuration.
    1. Run sizer to determine new kernel configuration

       /usr/sbin/sizer -n <filename>
       cp /tmp/<filename> .

       where <filename> is the name of the resulting file in /tmp

    2. Modify kernel configuration file

       vi <filename>

    3. Rebuild kernel

       /usr/sbin/doconfig -c <filename>
       n <CR> no you don't want to edit configuration files. The kernel
          will now rebuild. Just wait this take a few minutes.

    4. Copy new kernel to root directory.

       cp /sys/<filename>/vmunix /vmunix

5.  Reboot to single user.

    shutdown -h now
    b -fl s
    mount -a

6.  Remove old network setting and add new ones.

    /usr/sbin/netsetup

    Use tool to remove existing tu0 configurations and add configuration
    for tu1.

7.  Reboot computer.
 
    reboot
---------------------------------------------------------------

In addition to the notes by Andy, I have a few comments to add. 

Our DEC rep could not understand my attitude that we should be given
free support for installing a DEC network card in a DEC computer running
a DEC OS. She told me that this is not industry practice, and my
response that we had excellent free support (both advice and software)
from Gateway and Sun to upgrade those boxes to 100BASE-TX didn't change
her stated opinion. 

No one we spoke to at DEC seemed to find anything terribly wrong that
the 100BASE-TX card from DEC comes with absolutely no instructions or
software for using it with any operating system written by DEC. However,
if you want to put it in a PC, it does include software and instructions
for that.

If the person selling you the DEC 100BASE-TX card says all you do is
pull out the 10BASE-T card, pop in the 100BASE-TX card, and reboot, tell
them you aren't interested in buying any swamp land in Florida. For one
thing, the SCSI controller is integrated on the same card with the
10BASE-T! You can't remove it.

You seem to be compelled to use DEC 100BASE-TX NIC's with OSF. Our 3COM
rep said they don't support that OS.

For what it's worth, an explanation I heard for the required PROM and OS
upgrade is that the newer DEC 100BASE-TX cards such as the 500AA may
have larger FIFO's. Having too small a FIFO can result in buffer
overruns, and fast CPU's combined with fast NIC'S running telnet
sessions supposedly create ideal characteristics to elicit overrun. This
remark was from a non-DEC source. In any event, the older DEC 500XA
cards (which are no longer available) don't seem to require an upgrade
to 3.2G.

Thanks to everyone here who responded to my original plea for help. We
would not have gotten this far without the support we received from this
forum. Please let me know of any corrections or additions.

Robin 
