On Wed, 11 Jun 1997, Barry Roomberg wrote:

> I'm trying to determine if Digital Unix / Alpha
> is the appropriate solution for my current task,

I've found DU to be right for almost any task, unless the cost is an
issue.

> Since 32bit systems max out at 2 GB files, I obviously need a 64bit
> solution. 

Bzzt, try again.  There's nothing that says that file offsets have to be
the same size as the machine's native word.  Just as you can do 128-bit IP
addressing on a 32-bit platform, you can have on-the-order-of-64-bit files
on a 32-bit system.  Granted you can't mmap them, since memory pointers do
have to be in the machine's native format, but you can read, write, and
seek through them.

> Sun Solaris
> is on a 64bit "path", but currently only raw disk slices
> of > then 2GB are supported, and then only with
> explicit application support, handling the data
> in 32bit chunks at a time (sound like old 16bit DOS
> offset code!).

Solaris 2.6 has 64-bit file sizes, I believe.  Again, you can't mmap them,
but you can seek them.

Also, there are other 64-bit solutions.  There's the Sparc Ultra.
Unfortunately, Solaris isn't 64-bit native yet.  Fortunately, SparcLinux
is.  8^)  There's also a 64-bit version of the MIPS.  I would recommend
the Alphas, but wouldn't really recommend these alternatives.

> There are a variety of Alpha clone boxes out there,
> costing around 1/2 of the equivelant "pure" Digital
> system.  I'm looking to spend around $10,000, for both
> hardware and OS. Do any of you have any experience with
> these vendors/boxes, and can you make any recommendations,
> or would this approach be a stupid waste of time and
> eventually money?

The clone makers put out some pretty good boxes.  That thing to watch is
whether they skimp on the memory subsystem.  Digital's motherboards are
very good in this respect, but some cheap clone makers don't go so far,
resulting in artificially limiting the CPU's performance.  Maybe others
can address the clone v. authentic argument.

We use genuine DEC boxes, and we love them.

