Message-ID: <3397D36A.794B@creatis.insa-lyon.fr>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 246501751
References: <5mlkva$niu@drn.zippo.com> <5mnq8d$7k0@gateway.buttafuoco.net>
To: Jim Buttafuoco <root@192.255.255.255>, nasser@apldbio.com
Organization: CNRS (UMR 5515) Lyon - France
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Jim Buttafuoco wrote:
> 
> nasser,
>         There is a patch for this problem. Contact digital for the patch location

You can do the following :

Do this to determine if you are running into this bug:

su

dbx -k /vmunix
p db_enable

if db_enable is set to 1, then you have most likely built the kernel
with KDEBUG set.  This in turn sets db_enable to 1.  Somewhere in the
kernel, db_enable is causing the system to crash as it takes a path
it would not take if db_enable were 0.

The workaround -  set db_enable to 0.

To fix the running kernel

dbx -k /vmunix
assign db_enable = 0

To fix the disk copy (so you don't have to redo the live kernel after a
reboot)

dbx /vmunix          /* Important!  No -k here
patch db_enable = 0

(Information from DEC)


 Cordialement,
 Bernard.

