In article <3397796E.3AB654C0@lawrence.no.spam.edu>, Robert Lowe
<Robert.H.Lowe@lawrence.no.spam.edu> wrote:

>Just out of curiosity, does anyone know how to mount an AdvFS root
>parition while booted off of the CDrom?  Something like mount -t advfs
>root_domain#root /mnt fails since there is frame of reference for the
>domain information.  Obviously it's possible, or the system couldn't
>bootstrap itself, so it cannot be entirely dependent on /etc/fdmns.

I don't know if there is a preferred way, or something official, but I can
think of a hack way of doing it.

Mount a scratch UFS file system on top of /etc/fdmns (or a memory file
system, or anything writable).  Now do a mkdir /etc/fdmns/domain_name and
then create a symbolic link to the device containing the root partition you
wish to mount.

Again, I don't know if there is a better way, but this at least one way to
hack your way into mounting the domain.

I have not tested this to see if it actually works.   Your milage may vary.

