Agree.  I recently bought 2 small alpha servers running Digital Unix.  From
a starting point of being pretty sure what I wanted to buy was exactly that
(i.e. 2 alphas running Unix to be used at network servers) and having a
budget ($20,000), my goal was to find the most machine I could get for that
amount of money.  Sounds pretty simple, right?  The process dragged on for
weeks.  I couldn't find anyplace where all the available systems and options
and prices were just laid out so I could see what was what.  I had to go
through this iterative process with a dealer, guessing what process and
configuration I wanted, having him work up a quote, send me the quote, go
back and say, "what If we changed this to that?" and get a new quote.  I
managed to download bits a pieces of the Systems and Options catalog from
DEC's web site, but never quite got the big picture.

In the end, the configuration we ended up with was not quite what we
intended.  There was one mistake we made in writing up our purchase order
which ended up with us ordering the box with a 1Gig instead of a 2Gig
primary disk.  There was another mistake made by the dealer, where the
secondary disk was shipped configured wrong.  Both of these errors were
directly due (in my opinion) to the plethora of similar-sounding options and
part numbers, and lack of clear information describing it all.  It's far too
complex, and you're bound to make some minor mistakes after wading through a
weeks-long configuration process.
