Yes, this appears to be a DEC C compiler bug and we will be very
interested in getting this resolved. It would be best if you could
send us a standalone program the demonstrates this problem, along
with the command line and the UNIX version that you are running.

If your program inclues header files, it's generally easier for us
if you can preprocess the file and send us the preprocessed output.
You can mail that file to the address below or send/post a pointer
to a file available via ftp.

Below is an example of how to create a preprocessed file.

% cc $DFLAGS $IFLAGS -P t.c
% cat -r t.i > crash.c
% cc $CFLAGS crash.c

The first step produces a preprocessed file using your header files
and your define/include flags. This should give you a standalone
module that will reproduce the problem. The second step removes
blank lines from the output, making it easier to read/mail. The
third step is just to verify that the problem still does exist once
you try compileing the preprocessed file with the flag that causes
the problem (in your case "-O4 -inline speed").

We believe these issues reported by users deserve very high priority.
Thanks for reporting it.

-Jeff

