Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1



In article <33938584.796E@ova.comp.cz>, Instar Ostrava <insta@ova.comp.cz> writes:

>I use "ufs" file system on my Alpha Digital Unix 3.2 system and  I need
>to increase disk I/O performance.
>
>Is it using AdvFS a good idea ?

Maybe.  Maybe not.  The last time I ran Bonnie side by side, the two
were nearly the same.  As I recollect (a couple of versions ago)
UFS had a slight advantage for bandwidth.  When a particular I/O
load is well served by the log nature, then AdvFS can have an
advantage.  If you can live with file level striping, a multi-disk
domain under AdvFS can also provide better performance for 
individual files that are striped.

"increase disk I/O performance" covers a lot of ground.  Do you need
higher bandwidth or higher through-put?  Is your load read intensive,
write intensive or a combination of both?  Sequential or random I/Os?
Single-threaded or multi-threaded I/O?

Since the file systems are probably very close in performance for
most things you probably want to look at other ways to improve
the performance; faster disks, controller or host based striping,
etc.

