In article <5n1ks0$6kp@nntpd2.cxo.dec.com>, I (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.) wrote:
>
>In article <33938584.796E@ova.comp.cz>, Instar Ostrava <insta@ova.comp.cz> writes:
>
>>I use "ufs" file system on my Alpha Digital Unix 3.2 system and  I need
>>to increase disk I/O performance.
>>
>>Is it using AdvFS a good idea ?
>
>Maybe.  Maybe not.  The last time I ran Bonnie side by side, the two
>were nearly the same.

Well that was a couple of versions ago.  I had a bit of spare time today
so I set up a pair of disks on a AlphaServer 2100 running Digital UNIX
V4.0B.  The disks were both RZ28Ms on the internal SCSI adapter.  I created
a UFS and AdvFS file system on each using no options.  I used an older
version of the Bonnie I/O benchmark.  The file size selected was twice
the size of physical memory for the system.  I ran the each test twice,
re-createing the particular file system on the other disk.  The system
was running multi-user, but fiarly idle.

              -------Sequential Output-------- ---Sequential Input-- --Random--
              -Per Char- --Block--- -Rewrite-- -Per Char- --Block--- --Seeks---
Machine    MB K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU K/sec %CPU  /sec %CPU
UFS       256  4294 96.5  5122 21.2  2524 11.5  4458 87.7  5461 19.2 113.5  4.7
UFS       256  4273 95.9  5218 24.7  2574 12.4  4537 89.4  5403 19.1 117.6  5.2
AdvFS     256  4295 96.3  5553 28.1  3008 16.1  4732 94.2  7812 30.7 131.8  5.7
AdvFS     256  4237 96.2  5544 28.1  3076 17.5  4675 94.1  7799 31.1 132.0  6.0

For the block I/O loads, AdvFS was consistently and notably faster.  Bonnie
concentrates on sequential I/O loads, though it does have the random I/O
part.  If single stream I/O of this sort is what you're doing this should
offer reasonable insight as to what you can expect from AdvFS.  If your
I/O load is significantly different, then you'll have to do what I did;
benchmark.

