Michael Rys wrote:
> 
> I need to port a Sun Solaris program (C/C++) to a DEC OSF1 environment.
> The Sun version uses Sun's mutex_init(&sem, USYNC_PROCESS) which allows
> to use the
> mutex in shared memory between to processes. Newer versions of Solaris
> now support
> POSIX's pthread_mutex_init without losing any functionality.
> Fine, I thought to myself, both OS are POSIX compliant so it should be
> no problem
> to port. Wrong! pthread.h on OSF1 has different calls (and even worse),
> does not
> seem to provide the inter-process functionality...

First off, you don't say what version of "OSF1" you're using. However,
your symptoms, and the use of "DEC OSF1" and "OSF1" rather than "Digital
UNIX" lead me to assume that you're using 3.2x or earlier. Those older
versions were released before there was any such thing as "POSIX
threads" (the standard was approved in June, 1995). They implement DCE
threads, a loose interpretation of an early draft of the standard. There
are many syntactical differences between DCE threads and POSIX threads.

Digital UNIX 4.0 supports POSIX threads, as well as the obsolete DCE
threads (for the DCE product and for binary compatibility).

However, there's another issue. "POSIX conformance" doesn't help you in
this case, because what you want is an OPTIONAL feature of the standard.
Although Digital will implement the _POSIX_THREAD_PROCESS_SHARED option
in the future, we don't yet. It hasn't been as important as a number of
other features.

> So I have the following questions to the experts:
> 
> 1. How do I get the mutex behaviour on OSF1 for Sun's  mutex_init(&sem,
> USYNC_PROCESS)?

You can't. Use a semaphore.

> 2. Is there some predefined compilation flag which tells my compiler
> (g++), if I am
>    running OSF (similar to IBM's _AIX)? I tried __OSF__, __OSF1__ and
> _OSF_SOURCE -
>    none worked.

If you want a "predefined" compilation flag, that's up to the compiler.
DEC C and DEC C++ predefine __alpha and __osf__. The g++ compiler on my
system defines the same symbols.

